		{
			"id": "Tp1glHaBkZqrgvfkq",
			"name": "Custodian Oath and Mindset",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Custodians are famously remote even from other Imperial elites - Inquisitors, Chapter Masters and High Lords alike are received with cool, absolute courtesy rather than camaraderie - and Wraight's novels repeatedly frame this as focus rather than arrogance: nothing matters to a Custodian except the duty in front of him. The historic vow keeping the Legio on Terra is well documented in current Codex background and its lifting during the Indomitus Crusade is likewise current-edition canon, not a house rule.\n\nDesign: Duty (-15) and Sense of Duty (-5) mirror the Astartes package's structure but narrow the in-group to the Legio Custodes itself, per the \"aloof even from fellow elites\" note. Code of Honor (-10) is written from scratch rather than reusing Codex Astartes' text, since a Custodian answers to no Chapter. The Vow is included but disabled by default for the reason given above; enabling it is a straight -10 swap, nothing else in this file needs to change. Aloof (-1 Quirk) is the cheapest possible flag for the \"doesn't warm to outsiders\" personality note repeated across the fiction, kept small because nothing in the sources supports a harsher social disadvantage (Custodians aren't shown as cruel or friendless, just focused). Net: -15-5-10-1 = -31 with the Vow disabled, -41 enabled.",
			"tags": [
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Custodes"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tAzZQwJzhqmCXv0k8",
					"name": "Duty (The Emperor and the Throneworld)",
					"reference": "B133",
					"local_notes": "Almost all the time, Extremely Hazardous.",
					"base_points": -15
				},
				{
					"id": "tcrDeW6cs2SShF3dd",
					"name": "Sense of Duty (The Ten Thousand)",
					"reference": "B153",
					"local_notes": "Small group: fellow Custodians and the Throne they guard, not the wider Imperium.",
					"base_points": -5
				},
				{
					"id": "tVHC56E_Mmx-oj9ac",
					"name": "Code of Honor (Custodian's Creed)",
					"reference": "B127",
					"local_notes": "Never leave a post while the Emperor lives; never speak falsely to the Throne; accept correction from no authority outside the Legio Custodes and the Emperor himself.",
					"base_points": -10
				},
				{
					"id": "tZo-splxPOddvtPm7",
					"name": "Vow (Never Leave the Emperor's Side / Terra)",
					"reference": "B161",
					"local_notes": "Major Vow. Disabled by default: this project follows current Codex background and the Indomitus Crusade-era novels, where Guilliman as Lord Commander formally released Custodians for wider deployment after ten thousand years of confinement to Terra. Enable this for a Custodian bound by the older, absolute reading of the vow (pre-Indomitus, or a GM who wants the classic \"the Ten Thousand never leave Terra\" status quo).",
					"base_points": -10,
					"disabled": true
				},
				{
					"id": "te4OfjrB8QADSSssY",
					"name": "Aloof",
					"local_notes": "Rarely engages with anyone outside the Legio Custodes as an equal, the Emperor excepted.",
					"tags": [
						"Quirk"
					],
					"base_points": -1
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tbvVkBnE0wmMM-6Be",
			"name": "Rank (Legio Custodes)",
			"reference": "B29",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Every Custodian outranks nearly the entire Imperial military and ecclesiastical hierarchy by simple virtue of standing at the Emperor's side; Codex background and Wraight's novels both show even Inquisitors and Chapter Masters deferring to a Custodian's word on matters of the Throneworld's security.\n\nDesign: Rank (B29), 5 points/level, 6 levels = 30 points: double the Battle-Brother template's Rank 3 (15 points), reflecting that even a rank-and-file Custodian Guard carries more institutional weight than a veteran Astartes sergeant.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Custodes"
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 6
		},
		{
			"id": "ts7uSShM8FE0B3H7K",
			"name": "Rank (Shield-Captain, Legio Custodes)",
			"reference": "B29",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Shield-Captain commands a Custodian Guard shield-host and answers only to the Captain-General and the Emperor himself; current Codex background and Wraight's novels (which feature named Shield-Captains prominently) place the role above even a Chapter Master in Imperial precedence when Throneworld security is at stake.\n\nDesign: Rank (B29), 5 points/level, 9 levels = 45 points: three levels above the rank-and-file Custodian Guardian's own Rank 6 (30 points), reflecting genuine command authority over other Custodians rather than simple elite standing.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Custodes"
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 9
		},
		{
			"id": "tR9eM9srbQq0fARda",
			"name": "Reputation (A Shield-Captain of the Ten Thousand)",
			"reference": "B27",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Shield-Captain's heraldry marks him as one of the handful of Custodians trusted with independent command; background shows even Inquisitors and High Lords deferring further to a Shield-Captain than to an ordinary Custodian Guard.\n\nDesign: Reputation (B27), priced above the Guardian template's own Reputation (10 points for +3) at 15 points for a +4 reaction, following the same eyeballed-against-precedent approach used throughout this project's Reputation traits.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Custodes"
			],
			"base_points": 15,
			"features": [
				{
					"type": "reaction_bonus",
					"situation": "from anyone in the Imperium who recognizes a Shield-Captain's rank markings",
					"amount": 4
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tD0yz7l_d55xjRupm",
			"name": "Reputation (The Emperor's Companions)",
			"reference": "B27",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Custodian's golden plate is recognized on sight across the Imperium as the Emperor's own guard - awe, terror or reverent relief depending on who's looking - a stronger and more universal version of the reaction an Astartes' Chapter heraldry gets.\n\nDesign: Reputation (B27), priced informally above the Battle-Brother template's own Reputation (Angel of Death, 5 points for a +2 reaction) at 10 points for a +3 reaction recognized by essentially everyone in the Imperium, not just soldiery - the same design shortcut the Astartes file uses rather than a full frequency/scope calculation, since GURPS Reputation pricing is explicitly meant to be eyeballed against comparable published examples.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Custodes"
			],
			"base_points": 10,
			"features": [
				{
					"type": "reaction_bonus",
					"situation": "from anyone in the Imperium who recognizes Custodian gold, at any range they can be seen",
					"amount": 3
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "Tj8q3Cf5lgwnuYGaV",
			"name": "Pariah Gene (Blank)",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A blank is born, not made: a rare genetic absence that leaves a person's soul all but invisible in the warp, and makes the warp itself recoil from them. Psykers, daemons, and even animals find blanks instinctively unsettling to be near — a \"hole\" where a presence should be. Depicted consistently across Black Library (the Culexus Temple's \"Untouchables,\" Cain-series and Eisenhorn-cycle blanks) and Dark Heresy/Only War's Untouchable career.\n\nDesign: Deliberately carries no Warp Sorcery or Perils of the Warp modifier, and needs no Warp Empowerment: a Pariah's field is the *absence* of the warp, not a channelled act, so none of the framework's activation risk or spell-cost cap applies. See each child trait for its own derivation. Net for the package: +12 (Null Aura) +10 (Immunity) -3 (Unnerving Void) -10 (Social Stigma) = +9.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tzXBXI8nCMVyRvxfG",
					"name": "Null Aura",
					"local_notes": "Lore: The blank's presence itself smothers nearby psychic workings — the basis for why the Inquisition uses blanks to hunt witches and why daemons fear and hate them.\n\nDesign: Built on Affliction (Basic B35): base 10, the delivered \"Warp- Choked\" disadvantage is valued at -30 points for the enhancement math (+30%), stacked with Area Effect (+50%, B102, 2-yard radius), Always On (-40%, B113, it can't be switched off or aimed), and Emanation (-20%, B111, no ranged attack roll, just proximity).",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Psyker",
						"Imperium"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mOACgEmv_ZrNR6H3z",
							"name": "Disadvantage Delivered (Warp-Choked",
							"local_notes": "Any Warp-tagged ability used in the area must be rolled again as if failed; already-running continuous Warp effects go inert while inside it.",
							"cost_adj": "+30%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mXehSB_G2HzgMp7PA",
							"name": "Area Effect, 2 yards",
							"cost_adj": "+50%"
						},
						{
							"id": "m8CQtRu5wuwhS6zLg",
							"name": "Always On",
							"cost_adj": "-40%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mSvywWBSYr9Ow-Ktt",
							"name": "Emanation",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						}
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "trDAYALRZdyBgLIG2",
					"name": "Immunity to Warp-Based Effects",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Warp-based Affliction, Mind Control, Mind Probe, and Fear simply do not work on a Pariah.\n\nDesign: Resistant, Immunity tier (Basic B80: base rarity value x5). Warp- based attacks are treated as an \"occasional\" specific hazard (base 2) for a Pariah, since it is their entire defining trait, giving 2 x 5 = 10.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Psyker",
						"Imperium"
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "tzdD3cJ2DQ3ZIYah8",
					"name": "Unnatural Features (Unnerving Void)",
					"local_notes": "Lore: People and animals find a Pariah's presence quietly, deeply wrong.\n\nDesign: Basic's Unnatural Features (per-level reaction penalty), 3 levels of -1 each.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Social",
						"Psyker",
						"Imperium"
					],
					"points_per_level": -1,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 3
				},
				{
					"id": "tvKHb-uZo3ErCX18d",
					"name": "Social Stigma (Feared Blank)",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Feared and, in many sectors, watched or conscripted by the Inquisition and the Officio Assassinorum.\n\nDesign: Basic's Social Stigma (Minority Group), -10 (B155).",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Social",
						"Psyker",
						"Imperium"
					],
					"base_points": -10
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tZ1-Pwkdw98iEkBGO",
			"name": "Vow of Silence (Oath of the Silent Sisterhood)",
			"reference": "B161",
			"local_notes": "Major Vow: never speak aloud, under any circumstance. A Sister of Silence can still make non-verbal sounds (a battle-cry grunt, a warning shout with no words in it) and can write; she cannot use spoken language, ever, by her own binding oath rather than physical incapacity.\n\nLore: The Sisters of Silence take a formal vow of silence on joining the Sisterhood, communicating instead through their own battle-sign; the practice is widely depicted (Codex: Adeptus Custodes background, which covers the Sisters as the Custodes' sister order, and the Horus Heresy-era novel The Master of Mankind, where Sisters of Silence hunt and confront rogue psykers on Terra) without a single canonical page citation this project can point to.\n\nDesign: Modelled as a Vow (B161, Major, -10) rather than the involuntary Cannot Speak (Mute) disadvantage (-25): a Sister of Silence can physically speak and her pariah nature has nothing to do with her vocal cords, she simply never does, by oath - \"Mute by vow,\" per the framework's own phrasing, is exactly what a self-imposed Vow models, while the involuntary Basic Set Mute disadvantage would misrepresent the cause. Major (not Minor or Great) because it affects nearly every social and command interaction outside the Sisterhood's own sign language, but doesn't prevent effective communication outright.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium",
				"Sisters of Silence"
			],
			"base_points": -10
		},
		{
			"id": "tgkYl-CIoBHGxO163",
			"name": "Silent Sisterhood Battle-Sign",
			"reference": "B24",
			"local_notes": "Language (Silent Sisterhood Battle-Sign): Sign-only, Accented.\n\nLore: The Sisters' own hand-and-body sign language, drilled into every Sister as her primary means of tactical communication in place of vox or speech; referenced throughout Custodes-adjacent background as distinct from ordinary military hand signals.\n\nDesign: Built as a Language (B23-24) using the sign-language variant of the standard cost table rather than the spoken/written columns, at Accented fluency (4 points): fluent enough for real tactical use but, per the framework's citation-honesty rule, this project has no source claiming Sisters are drilled to native perfect fluency versus merely very good working fluency.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium",
				"Sisters of Silence"
			],
			"base_points": 4
		},
		{
			"id": "tA0KkMpJ_h9rhrczF",
			"name": "Sense of Duty (The Silent Sisterhood)",
			"reference": "B153",
			"local_notes": "Large group: the Sisterhood as a whole, not just one Sister's own squad.\n\nLore: Sisters of Silence operate as a tight, self-contained order bound to each other and to their hunt for rogue psykers and daemonic incursion; background consistently shows them protecting and recovering their own before anything else.\n\nDesign: Sense of Duty (B153), Large Group, -10 points, as the modifier table gives for an order-sized in-group.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium",
				"Sisters of Silence"
			],
			"base_points": -10
		},
		{
			"id": "tOwNffKnlNvd-bmGO",
			"name": "Intolerance (Psykers)",
			"reference": "B140",
			"local_notes": "Occasional scope: psykers are a small fraction of the Imperium's population.\n\nLore: A Sister of Silence's pariah nature makes her instinctively wary of, and unwelcoming to, psykers - the profession the Sisterhood exists to police - though background stops short of showing them cruel to psykic Imperial citizens who pose no threat.\n\nDesign: Intolerance (B140) at the \"Occasional\" scope modifier (-2 points): psykers are rare in the general population even though a Sister encounters them often professionally, and B140's scope size is about real-world prevalence of the disliked group, not how often the character's job brings her into contact with it.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium",
				"Sisters of Silence"
			],
			"base_points": -2,
			"features": [
				{
					"type": "reaction_bonus",
					"situation": "from psykers who sense the dislike",
					"amount": -1
				}
			]
		}
	]
}
